# The ledger of `math/complex`, the complex numbers and their functions. Its
# blocks were `math.ledger`'s until 2026-11-20, when the coherence review's D16
# made the complex numbers a subpackage at layer 1 and dropped the `_complex`
# its names carried in `math`'s face: `math.add_complex` is `math/complex.add`,
# `math.to_complex` is `math/complex.from_polar`. What each function answers
# did not move.

capability complex/type  a complex number as a value: its two parts, built and read
  heroes  math/complex.Complex
  go      complex, real, imag, complex128, complex64 (built-ins)
  rust    -
  python  complex, complex.real, complex.imag, complex.from_number
  odin    complex128, complex64, complex32, real, imag (built-ins)
  zig     std.math.Complex, Complex.init
  note    a record of two `f64`s, as `big.Decimal` is a record: this block was refused in `numbers.ledger` as `number/complex` for "the language has no complex type", and a record with functions is the type. Rust's standard library has none; its `num-complex` crate is outside it. The text of a complex number is the block `complex/text` below

capability complex/arithmetic  the sum, the difference, the product, the quotient and the negation
  heroes  math/complex.add, math/complex.subtract, math/complex.multiply, math/complex.divide, math/complex.negate
  go      + - * / on complex128
  rust    -
  python  complex.__add__, __sub__, __mul__, __truediv__, __neg__, __pos__, __radd__, __rsub__, __rmul__, __rtruediv__
  odin    + - * / on complex128
  zig     Complex.add, sub, mul, div, neg
  note    C11 Annex G's: the product and the quotient recover an infinity where the plain formula answers a nan in both parts, as Python 3.15 does, and a zero divisor answers an infinity where Python raises. The quotient scales both numbers exactly to one binade and takes each sum of two products with a fused multiply-add, so a number over itself is exactly 1; held to the exact quotient within 4 units in the last place at 64 edge pairings and 24 pairs from the subnormal to the overflow, where Python's quotient answered `(1.5 - 0.5i) / (1.5 - 0.5i)` as `1 - 1.67e-17i` and missed a subnormal quotient in its 12th digit (run 2026-11-17). Repaired 2026-11-18 (repairs-3): on the Windows box the quotient missed two of those edge pairings, `0 / (nan + inf i)` and `-0 / (inf + nan i)`, a zero's sign. Not a C call of the UCRT: Annex G's recovery of a finite number over an infinity takes the sign of a nan part of the divisor (`copysign(0.0, nan)`), and no operation promises a nan's sign: this Mac's `INF - INF` has it clear, x86-64's has it set (Intel's "indefinite"; the box's own reading is owed). With every nan of the cases given the sign set, this Mac missed exactly those two cases. Python 3.15.2 and Go 1.27 here both follow the nan's sign (`0 / (nan + inf i)` is `0 + 0i` with the sign clear and `0 - 0i` with it set, asked of each). Here a nan part counts as a plain zero whatever its sign, so the answer is Python's for its `float("nan")` on every platform, and the generated cases of `mul` and `div` are asked with every nan's sign set and clear

capability complex/conjugate  the conjugate
  heroes  math/complex.conjugate
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  complex.conjugate
  odin    conj (built-in)
  zig     Complex.conjugate, std.math.complex.conj

capability complex/size-and-angle  the size and the angle of a complex number
  heroes  math/complex.abs, math/complex.phase
  go      cmplx.Abs, cmplx.Phase
  rust    -
  python  complex.__abs__, abs, cmath.phase
  odin    abs (built-in), cmplx.phase
  zig     std.math.complex.abs, arg, Complex.magnitude, Complex.squaredMagnitude
  note    the squared size is `re * re + im * im`, which needs no name

capability complex/polar  a complex number by its size and its angle, and back
  heroes  math/complex.Polar, math/complex.to_polar, math/complex.from_polar
  go      cmplx.Polar, cmplx.Rect
  rust    -
  python  cmath.polar, cmath.rect
  odin    cmplx.polar, cmplx.rect_complex128
  zig     -
  note    Python's answers at 49 pairings of edges and 16 random ones (run 2026-11-17); where `rect` raises, an infinite angle beside a size that is not zero, an infinite size answers `inf + nan i`, as Python answers it at a nan angle, and a finite one `nan + nan i`

capability complex/exp-and-log  e raised to a complex number, and the logarithms
  heroes  math/complex.exp, math/complex.ln, math/complex.log10, math/complex.log
  go      cmplx.Exp, cmplx.Log, cmplx.Log10
  rust    -
  python  cmath.exp, cmath.log, cmath.log10
  odin    cmplx.exp, cmplx.ln, cmplx.log10
  zig     std.math.complex.exp, log
  note    the logarithm of zero is minus the infinity and an overflowing `exp` an infinity, Annex G's values, where Python raises. The real part of `math/complex.ln` is held to the exact logarithm, 80 digits of Python's `decimal`, which `cmath.log` misses by 30% at `0.6 + 0.8i` (2.886579864025407e-17 for 2.2204460492503132e-17) and `cmath.log10` by up to 3 units in the last place (run 2026-11-17)

capability complex/sqrt-and-pow  the square root and the powers
  heroes  math/complex.sqrt, math/complex.pow
  go      cmplx.Sqrt, cmplx.Pow
  rust    -
  python  cmath.sqrt, complex.__pow__, __rpow__, pow
  odin    cmplx.sqrt, cmplx.pow
  zig     std.math.complex.sqrt, pow
  note    a whole real exponent below 2 to the 63rd in size is multiplied out by squaring, each product held near 1 by a power of two kept apart, so `(1 + 1i)^2` is exactly `2i` where C's `cpow` answers `2^-53 + 2i` on this machine, and only correctly rounded operations are taken, so the answer is the same on every platform; Python multiplies out only to 100, and this function did so until 2026-11-18, when the Linux arm64 container answered the powers at 101 up to 121 units of their last place away from this Mac's: the polar form there goes through `pow`, `atan2`, `cos` and `sin`, which glibc and Apple round differently, and was 24 to 77 units of the size's last place from the true power on both, where squaring is 1.5 to 34. The generated test holds every power to the true one, mpmath at 600 bits, within `4 |n|` units of the last place of its size for a whole `n` and 4 for any other exponent (3 at most measured on either platform). Zero to a power is 1 for a zero exponent (Python's, where C's `cpow(0, 0)` is a nan), zero for a positive real part, `inf + nan i` for a negative one and a nan for an imaginary one, where Python raises

capability complex/trigonometry  the sine, the cosine and the tangent
  heroes  math/complex.sin, math/complex.cos, math/complex.tan
  go      cmplx.Sin, cmplx.Cos, cmplx.Tan
  rust    -
  python  cmath.sin, cmath.cos, cmath.tan
  odin    cmplx.sin, cmplx.cos, cmplx.tan
  zig     std.math.complex.sin, cos, tan
  note    as Annex G defines them, a quarter turn of the hyperbolic ones, so their edges are those turned

capability complex/inverse-trigonometry  the inverse sine, cosine and tangent, their branch cuts on the axes
  heroes  math/complex.asin, math/complex.acos, math/complex.atan
  go      cmplx.Asin, cmplx.Acos, cmplx.Atan
  rust    -
  python  cmath.asin, cmath.acos, cmath.atan
  odin    cmplx.asin, cmplx.acos, cmplx.atan
  zig     std.math.complex.asin, acos, atan
  note    Kahan's formulas (1987); `atan(i)` is `i inf`, Annex G's value, where Python raises and Apple's C library answers `pi/4 + i inf`

capability complex/hyperbolic  the hyperbolic sine, cosine and tangent
  heroes  math/complex.sinh, math/complex.cosh, math/complex.tanh
  go      cmplx.Sinh, cmplx.Cosh, cmplx.Tanh
  rust    -
  python  cmath.sinh, cmath.cosh, cmath.tanh
  odin    cmplx.sinh, cmplx.cosh, cmplx.tanh
  zig     std.math.complex.sinh, cosh, tanh
  note    `tanh(0 + inf i)` and `tanh(0 + nan i)` are `0 + nan i`, C17's DR 471, which Python follows for the nan and Apple's C library does not; where Annex G leaves a sign to the library, Python's: a nan part never turns the other part's sign

capability complex/inverse-hyperbolic  the inverse hyperbolic sine, cosine and tangent, their branch cuts on the axes
  heroes  math/complex.asinh, math/complex.acosh, math/complex.atanh
  go      cmplx.Asinh, cmplx.Acosh, cmplx.Atanh
  rust    -
  python  cmath.asinh, cmath.acosh, cmath.atanh
  odin    cmplx.asinh, cmplx.acosh, cmplx.atanh
  zig     std.math.complex.asinh, acosh, atanh
  note    `atanh(1)` is `inf + 0i`, Annex G's value, where Python raises and Apple's C library answers `inf + 0.785i`; `acosh(0 + nan i)` is `nan + i pi/2`, C17's DR 471 and Python's

capability complex/classify  whether a complex number is a nan, an infinity, or finite
  heroes  math/complex.is_nan, math/complex.is_inf, math/complex.is_finite
  go      cmplx.IsNaN, cmplx.IsInf
  rust    -
  python  cmath.isnan, cmath.isinf, cmath.isfinite
  odin    cmplx.is_nan, cmplx.is_inf
  zig     -
  note    `inf + nan i` is an infinity and not a nan, Go's reading and Annex G's, where Python's `isnan` calls it both: exactly one of the three holds of any complex number

capability complex/close  whether two complex numbers are close
  heroes  math/complex.is_close
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  cmath.isclose
  odin    -
  zig     -
  note    Python's rule, the tolerances named and given every time; a nan tolerance allows nothing, where ordering it would abort

capability complex/project  the point of the Riemann sphere a complex number stands for
  heroes  math/complex.project
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  odin    -
  zig     std.math.complex.proj
  note    C's `cproj`

capability complex/cotangent  the cotangent of a complex number
  heroes  refused: one `math/complex.divide` of 1 by `math/complex.tan`; neither Python nor C names it
  go      cmplx.Cot
  rust    -
  python  -
  odin    cmplx.cot
  zig     -

capability complex/edge-values  the infinity and the nan as complex numbers
  heroes  refused: they are built directly, `complex.Complex(re: math.INF, im: 0.0)`, and a constant may not hold a record
  go      cmplx.Inf, cmplx.NaN
  rust    -
  python  cmath.inf, cmath.infj, cmath.nan, cmath.nanj
  odin    cmplx.inf_complex128, cmplx.nan_complex128
  zig     -

capability complex/text  a complex number read from text and written as text
  heroes  pending
  go      strconv.ParseComplex, strconv.FormatComplex
  rust    -
  python  complex (from a text), complex.__format__, complex.__repr__
  odin    strconv.parse_complex128, parse_complex64, parse_complex32
  zig     -
  note    the number is `math/complex.Complex`, so its text is written beside it here, and `numbers.ledger`'s `number/complex` names this block. Until 2026-11-20 `numbers` and `math` were both layer 0 and neither could use the other; `math/complex` stands at layer 1 and may use `numbers`, so nothing but the work keeps it pending. Until it is written a program writes `numbers.format_float` of each part

dropped
  python  cmath.pi, cmath.e, cmath.tau
  reason  `cmath`'s copies of `math`'s constants, which are `math.PI`, `math.E` and `math.TAU`
  python  complex.__bool__, __complex__, __eq__, __hash__, __getnewargs__, __reduce__, __reduce_ex__
  reason  the language's own: `==` is structural on a record, and nothing has truthiness
  odin    cmplx.*_complex32, cmplx.*_complex64, complex32, complex64
  reason  every function again for parts of `f16` and `f32`: `math`'s floats are `f64`, and a narrower part is `to_f32` of the answer
  zig     std.math.complex.ldexp_cexp
  reason  `exp` times a power of two, by its name a step of `exp` near the overflow, which `math/complex.exp` takes inside
